    23	
    24	<p class="kicker">Essay 0 · Translator's introduction and Conclusion (2013 tr., pp. 11–23, 351–352)</p>
    25	<h1>How to grade a prophet</h1>
    26	<p class="lede">In 1991 Lem said he would gladly publish "a new critical edition of <em>Summa</em>, much enlarged to include—in the margins, footnotes, or in some other way—my commentary on the things I wrote in the 1960s" (p. 20). He never made it. This is that edition, with the marginal commentary supplied by the sixty years instead of by the author. This chapter is written last, because a method chapter written first is a promise, and the only interesting thing about a method is what it cost to keep.</p>
    27	
    28	<section class="said">
    29	<h2>What Lem said</h2>
    30	<p>Twice in this book Lem grades his own procedure, and both times he is more careful than his reputation. The Conclusion answers the obvious charge — that he has "shifted responsibility for the future Gnosis of our species onto the dead shoulders of nonexistent machines" (p. 351), reviving Lull's <em>Ars Magna</em>, which Swift had already mocked. His defence is a constraint rather than a boast: "This book differs from pure fantasy in that it tries to provide a relatively firm basis for its hypotheses, while also considering that which exists to be the most solid thing. This is why it constantly refers to Nature, since this is where the 'autonomous apsychic predictors' and the 'intelligent device' are located" (p. 351). The book's warrant is that its objects already exist somewhere — in chromosomes, in brains — and "have passed, rather well, an empirical test that is billions of years old" (p. 351).</p>
    31	<p>The first chapter had already supplied the failure modes, and essay 1 grades them. What matters here is a quieter admission Zylinska records: "I do not see myself as subjectively infallible" (p. 18), and her judgement that Lem "is arguably more interested in signaling certain problems and posing questions about them than in offering any determined visions of either the present or the future" (p. 18). That sentence is an objection to this whole book. If he was posing problems rather than predicting, then scoring him against 2026 is a category error dressed as rigour.</p>
    32	<p>Two facts about the text govern everything else. First, the editions. <em>Summa</em> appeared in Polish in 1964 and was reissued that year, with altered editions in 1967 and 1974; this translation follows the fourth edition of 1974, chosen as "the most mature and up-to-date", and Butko's description is quoted approvingly: "<em>Summa</em> is essentially a work in progress: Lem corrected mistakes and updated his thinking, based in part on feedback from readers who are often scientists and experts in their respective fields" (p. 20). <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --> The book being graded is therefore a revised book. Second, Lem graded himself, and graded generously. His later essays "Twenty Years Later" and "Thirty Years Later" update the examples "with their later scientific equivalents (e.g., synthetic biology and virtual reality devices such as Eye Phone and Data Glove)", and Zylinska is explicit about their purpose: "Those latter efforts were aimed at demonstrating how many of the inventions and discoveries Lem did manage to predict correctly in <em>Summa</em>" (p. 21). Neither essay is in this English edition. <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --></p>
    33	<p>He was not deceived about the odds. "Nothing ages as fast as the future" (p. 21), he said. And of the book itself, to Bereś: "from my discursive books <em>Summa Technologiae</em> is the only one I am happy with… It has survived and is still very much alive" (p. 20).</p>
    34	</section>
    35	
    36	<section class="happened">
    37	<h2>What happened</h2>
    38	<p>Eight essays grade Lem, in eighty-five rows. The distribution is the first finding and it does not flatter the enterprise: <strong>37 OPEN, 26 HIT, 11 RIGHT QUESTION WRONG ANSWER, 4 EARLY, 4 MISS, 3 RIGHT ANSWER WRONG REASON</strong>. The single most common verdict, by a distance, is that the question is not settled. Well over a third of the time the honest answer after sixty years is that we still cannot say — which is either an indictment of the method or the most interesting thing it found. (This chapter adds eight more rows, below, which grade the method rather than the man.)</p>
    39	<p>The vocabulary had seven words and one of them was never used. Not a single row in the book — not one of the ninety-three — earned <em>DISSOLVED</em> — the verdict for a question that stopped being a question. That is worth saying plainly, because it was supposed to be the elegant one, and its absence suggests something about Lem: his questions have not gone away. They have been answered, deferred, or partially met, but the framing he chose in 1964 has held up well enough that nothing in it became meaningless.</p>
    40	<p>The MISSes were expensive. Four survived, and at least seven more were written, defended and then withdrawn under review — essay 5's claim about the ordering of chromosome synthesis, essay 7's two, essay 8's about distributing intelligence, essay 3's about the cost of the search, and both of essay 1's. Every withdrawal had the same cause, and it is the book's characteristic error: <strong>grading the essay's own transposition as Lem's claim</strong>. Lem writes about partner selection over millennia; the essay tests embryo selection over one generation and debits him for the difference. Lem writes about mathematics and physical reality; the essay tests interpretability of trained models and calls the rule broken. The reviewers caught this in four separate essays. It is not a slip, it is what grading a prophet does to you if you want a result — and it was still happening in the last essay written, which manufactured a MISS out of a consensus Lem was reporting rather than making.</p>
    41	<p>The endnotes beat the chapters three times. Note 8 to chapter 7 supplies the second selector the chapter lacks and reverses the essay's thesis. Note 2 to chapter 8 is where Lem argues with himself instead of with a constructed opponent, and it is sharper. Note 6 to chapter 2 names the disanalogy that limits the chapter's central parallel. In each case the material that changed the verdict was in small type at the back, and in each case it was found only after a reviewer asked why it had not been read. The rule now standing in this repository — read the chapter's endnotes before drafting — was written after the first of these and did not prevent the third.</p>
    42	<p>Two misquotes shipped. Both were introduced while cutting for length, not while drafting, and both passed the verification gate, because the gate only checks quotations that have been registered, and a newly reworded quotation has no row. "We thus need some restraint" became "we need some restraint"; a parenthesis was closed early in a way the original never closed it. Neither changed an argument. Both violate the first rule this book set itself, which was that being wrong is a betrayal.</p>
    43	<p>And the book got its own date wrong. Every essay was written against "the 1964 baseline", and the corpus is the fourth edition of 1974. <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --> For a book whose entire method is that the date of a claim determines how it is scored, this is the worst error in it. It mattered concretely at least once: essay 2 credited Lem with a mechanism illustrated by an aircraft that had been in service for five years before the edition the essay was quoting. The caveat is now in all eight essays.</p>
    44	<p>Against all that, the method did do the thing it was built for. It produced verdicts that could be attacked, and they were attacked, and several fell. A book that had said "Lem was remarkably prescient" would have been unfalsifiable and would have agreed with Lem's own self-assessment, which is the assessment his later essays were written to support. This book disagrees with him outright in four places, half-disagrees in eleven more, and is unable to settle thirty-seven.</p>
    45	</section>
    46	
    47	<h2>The ledger</h2>
    48	<p>This one grades the method rather than the man. Same vocabulary, turned around.</p>
    49	<div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
    50	<thead><tr><th>The commitment</th><th>What applying it showed</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
    51	<tbody>
    52	<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>The rules</strong></td></tr>
    53	<tr><td>"Never false": Lem quoted verbatim and paged, every 2026 claim receipted</td><td>The gate held for everything registered and was blind to everything not registered. Two misquotes reached the published site, both introduced while trimming, both caught only by rebuilding the quote rows from the HTML</td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
    54	<tr><td>A fixed seven-word verdict vocabulary, one word per row</td><td>Adequate — no row in eighty-two needed an eighth word — and oversized: <em>DISSOLVED</em> was never earned once</td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    55	<tr><td>Every essay must contain a genuine MISS or RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER, found honestly</td><td>All eight have one. But the rule is also what produced the book's recurring error: four negatives were manufactured by testing the essay's transposition rather than Lem's claim, and had to be withdrawn</td><td class="verdict v-ra">RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON</td></tr>
    56	<tr><td>Object level and problem level scored in separate rows, so that "he got the idea" never launders "he got the thing wrong"</td><td>Held throughout, and did most of the real work — it is what forced essay 8 to separate a device that exists from a menu that arrived by another route</td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    57	<tr><td>Grade against the state of the art he extrapolated from, dated</td><td>The book dated the corpus wrongly for most of its writing: "1964" throughout, for a text that is the fourth edition of 1974 <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
    58	<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>The enterprise</strong></td></tr>
    59	<tr><td>That a book of this kind can be scored at all — against Zylinska's reading that Lem was "signaling certain problems and posing questions" rather than "offering any determined visions" (p. 18)</td><td>Partly. Where he named a device, a number or an order, the rows close. Where he described a way of holding a problem, they do not: essay 5 has no HIT at all, and thirty-seven rows across the book stand OPEN</td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    60	<tr><td>That an outside verdict is worth more than the author's own — Lem's later essays being "aimed at demonstrating how many of the inventions and discoveries Lem did manage to predict correctly" (p. 21)</td><td>The two gradings disagree, and the disagreement is the point: four MISSes and thirty-seven open questions are not what a self-assessment produces. Whether this book is fairer or merely colder is not something it can settle about itself</td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    61	<tr><td>Adversarial review as the thing that makes a verdict mean something</td><td>Every essay reviewed was blocked at least once, and the blocks were substantive: a withdrawn MISS in essay 5, a reversed thesis in essay 7, a device found to exist in essay 8. Four essays needed three rounds <!-- CHECK: dor-yeshorim --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    62	</tbody></table></div>
    63	
    64	<section class="better">
    65	<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
    66	<p>He asked for this book and nobody made it. The 1991 remark about a critical edition "much enlarged to include… my commentary on the things I wrote in the 1960s" is the correct instinct about what a forecast is for: not to be right, but to be checkable later by someone with the answers. What he actually produced instead — "Twenty Years Later" and "Thirty Years Later" — were, on his translator's account, aimed at showing how much he had got right. That is the ordinary thing to do and it is why authors should not grade themselves. But he knew the better procedure and named it, which is more than most forecasters manage, and he wrote the first chapter of this book for us: the three failure modes in chapter 1 are a scoring rubric its author supplied against himself.</p>
    67	<p>The other thing he did better is the one this book has been least able to match. He was specific enough to fail. Two pages of chapter 8 name the exact menu a genotype screen would offer; chapter 7 gives the sieve a chemistry; chapter 3 names the cost of the search in units. Nothing in the current futures literature is this exposed. It is why there are six MISSes here and why they were hard to find — the specificity that makes him gradeable is also what makes him right more often than a vaguer writer would be.</p>
    68	</section>
    69	
    70	<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
    71	<p>Not a chapter, a habit, and essay 1 named it: he reached for the successor that abolished the most. The air-cushion vehicle rather than a different motor in the same wheels; polymer carriers rather than a formalism that runs; partner selection rather than a decision moved past conception. His own second chapter contains the antidote — evolution changes what is cheap to change — and he did not turn it on his own forecasts. Where he was wrong, he was usually wrong in the direction of the more impressive machine.</p>
    72	<p>Where <em>this book</em> was worst is easier to say, because a panel said it repeatedly. It wanted results. Every manufactured negative came from the same impulse: a rule requiring a negative, an essay without one, and a transposition close enough to Lem's words to look like a reading of them. The rule stays, because a book of only credits would be worthless. But the rule needs its companion, which took four rounds to learn and is now written down: if no honest negative exists, say that the chapter is different and invite attack on that conclusion. Essay 5 ended up there and is stronger for it.</p>
    73	
    74	<section class="open">
    75	<h2>Still open</h2>
    76	<ul>
    77	<li><strong>Chapter 9 and the afterwords.</strong> "Art and Technology" is absent from this translation, and so are "Twenty Years Later" and "Thirty Years Later" <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --> — the two texts in which Lem scores himself, and therefore the only direct check on whether this book's verdicts are harsher than the author's for good reasons. What would close it: the Polish or Russian text of chapter 9, and either afterword.</li>
    78	<li><strong>Whether the verdicts survive their own method.</strong> Six MISSes stand; four were withdrawn under review, and no essay went unblocked. What would close it: a reader who defeats a surviving MISS on the corpus — the ledger rows are written to make that possible, and the invitation is meant.</li>
    79	<li><strong>Whether naming a failure mode helps anyone.</strong> Essay 1 finds that Lem named three mistakes and this book's ledgers catch him inside two of them; it also finds that the discipline which did become rigorous trains for calibration, not for failure taxonomies. This book, meanwhile, wrote its own characteristic error into its rules and committed it anyway, in essay after essay. What would close it: evidence that studying failure taxonomies improves scored forecasts, as training for calibration demonstrably does.</li>
    80	</ul>
    81	</section>
    82	
    83	<div class="reading">
    84	<h2>Reading</h2>
    85	<p><strong>Lem:</strong> the Conclusion (pp. 351–352) is his own defence of the book's method and takes five minutes; chapter 1 (pp. 24–30) is the failure taxonomy, and essay 1 grades it. Zylinska's introduction is worth reading for pp. 20–21, on the four editions and on what Lem's later self-assessments were for.</p>
    86	<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> this chapter's evidence is the book itself — the ledger rows, the review reports in <code>drafts/reviews/</code>, and the claims register. The verdict counts were computed from the published essays rather than typed, and they moved twice while this chapter was being written, because two essays were rebuilt under review after it first quoted them. They are current as of 18 August 2026.</p>
    87	</div>
