 succeeded in 169ms:
    35	<p>Note 2 (pp. 368–369) is where he argues with himself rather than a constructed opponent, and it is sharper. Against the absolutist: "An opponent of 'bioconstructionism' cannot limit himself to opposing any 'plans for man's redesign' but should rather give up on all of civilization's achievements, such as medicine and technology, and retreat to the forest on all four" (p. 369). Against himself: "Will the elimination of, say, epilepsy not get rid of both epileptics and Dostoyevskys?" (p. 368). And the landing: "The aura of moral responsibility must envelop the field of bioconstructionism", but "it is high time he took such a conscious and responsible risk… even if this knowledge remains incomplete" (p. 369). The baseline: the cyborg paper of 1960; computer date-matching at Stanford on an IBM 650 in 1959, then at scale as Operation Match from 1965; the genetic code being cracked; no way to read a genome — bearing in mind that this translation follows the altered fourth edition of 1974, so the text is mid-1960s as revised. <!-- CHECK: baseline-1964-ch8 --></p>
    36	</section>
    37	
    38	<section class="happened">
    39	<h2>What happened</h2>
    40	<p>The second stage was built, and it was built as a matchmaker. Dor Yeshorim has run since 1983: young people in Orthodox Jewish communities are tested and given an anonymous number, and when a match is proposed the two numbers are checked together, before the couple meets, returning one answer — proceed, or incompatible. It has screened hundreds of thousands of people across eleven countries. <!-- CHECK: dor-yeshorim --> That is Lem's p. 345 device, operating on the branch he called "a correct decision": serious recessive disease. It is not operating on the other two. Nobody is screened out for a low predicted intelligence or an unstable personality, and the programme is bounded to communities that asked for it.</p>
    41	<p>Outside those communities the second stage went somewhere else entirely — not to the matchmaker but one step down the reproductive pipeline, to the embryo. Companies sell polygenic scoring of IVF embryos; in June 2025 one launched a product ranking them on IQ, height, hair and eye colour, anxiety and ADHD alongside disease risk, from about $5,999. <!-- CHECK: nucleus-embryo-2025 --> <!-- CHECK: embryo-screening-2024 --> The trait list overlaps his and extends past it. This is a different device from the one he described, applied to a different object, and this essay counts it as a third path rather than as his second stage arriving late.</p>
    42	<p>The first stage — the psychosomatic matchmaker becoming general — is harder to score than it looks. Meeting online overtook meeting through friends around 2013 and is now the most common way US heterosexual couples meet, <!-- CHECK: online-dating-2019 --> but that is a finding about where people meet, not evidence that a compatibility algorithm chose for them. What certainly did not arrive is the second half of his sentence. No mass platform discourages a marriage or claims the authority to; none has become the norm against which a match could be a forbidden fruit. Dor Yeshorim is the exception that shows the condition: it acquired that authority because the community already had it, and Lem never named that as a requirement.</p>
    43	<p>The premise underneath was never established either way. Lem rests his forecast on the machine's "better knowledge" and flags his own evidence as limited. The best-known test since is narrower than it is usually reported: machine learning over more than a hundred self-reported traits could modestly predict how much a person would desire partners in general, and could not predict how two particular people would feel before meeting. <!-- CHECK: joel-2017 --> That is a result about initial attraction, not about whether matched marriages last, and the question he actually asked is still unanswered.</p>
    44	<p>The loud path ran the course his sentence about disgust predicts. He Jiankui announced CCR5-edited twins in November 2018; a third edited child was born the following year, confirmed by the court that convicted him in December 2019 and sentenced him to three years, with a lifetime ban from reproductive medicine. <!-- CHECK: he-jiankui-2018 --> In 2025 the major cell and gene therapy societies called for a ten-year moratorium on heritable editing — a professional call, not a ban. <!-- CHECK: germline-moratorium-2025 --> Cutting up bodies drew a prison sentence. Comparing two ID numbers over the telephone has drawn nothing.</p>
    45	<p>His deflations held. No population's average life expectancy is near a hundred; gains have decelerated since 1990 and radical extension is judged implausible this century without slowing aging itself. <!-- CHECK: olshansky-2024 --> No astronaut has had a digestive system removed; what exists is the "microattachments" clause — implanted intrathecal pumps, subdermal hormone implants. <!-- CHECK: implanted-pumps --> And on difficulty he was wrong in the direction he could not have checked: distributing high intelligence is not "relatively easy" by any known route. Selection among a couple's own embryos is bounded by the variance that segregates within one family — about 2.5 IQ points for the top of ten on current predictors, with wide intervals, though that figure rises as scores improve. <!-- CHECK: karavani-2019 --> Whether assortative matching over many generations could do more is exactly what his machine was for, and nobody has run it.</p>
    46	</section>
    47	
