    66	
    67	<section class="better">
    68	<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
    69	<p>He put the moral weight where it belongs, which almost nothing written since has done as plainly. Editing three children produced a criminal trial, a professional moratorium and years of argument. A telephone line that tells two people they may not marry has run for four decades and is discussed, when at all, as a public-health success. Lem saw in 1966 that this asymmetry was the problem itself — that disgust tracks the vividness of the procedure, not the size of its effect — and said so in one sentence about brains and marriage counselling. He also refused both easy exits. Note 2 will not let the objector keep medicine while rejecting bioconstruction, and it will not let the enthusiast pretend the risk is small: "Will the elimination of, say, epilepsy not get rid of both epileptics and Dostoyevskys?" (p. 368). And his moral grading inside the forecast has held up better than his sociology — he marked disability screening as correct and intelligence and personality as doubtful, which is exactly the line the built system respects and the sold product does not.</p>
    70	</section>
    71	
    72	<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
    73	<p>He assumed the machine would acquire authority by being right. The forecast is that once "better knowledge" is demonstrated the technology becomes "a cultural norm", after which a discouraged marriage is a forbidden fruit. Neither half happened where the machines actually went. The better knowledge was never demonstrated — the evidence he cites he calls limited, and it has not improved. And the platforms that matched hundreds of millions of people acquired no standing to discourage anyone; they rank and they queue, and a user who ignores them has broken no rule. The one place the norm exists, it was not earned by the machine at all: Dor Yeshorim works because a community that already had authority over marriage chose to route it through a test. That precondition is the load-bearing part, and it is the part he leaves out.</p>
    74	<p>He also assumed the unit of selection would stay the couple, which is why the trait branch went somewhere he was not looking. In vitro fertilisation moved the decision past conception, and the menu he wrote — intelligence, personality — is now sold as embryo scoring rather than partner screening. That is a third path, not his second stage arriving late, and this essay scores it as one. Whether it can do anything at population scale is unsettled; whether his own route could have done more, over the millennia he allowed it, nobody has tested and probably nobody will.</p>
    75	
    76	<section class="open">
    77	<h2>Still open</h2>
    78	<ul>
    79	<li><strong>Whether any of it moves a population.</strong> Neither the partner screening nor the embryo scoring has been shown to shift a trait distribution in a birth cohort. What would close it: a measured shift attributable to either — or a demonstration that within-family variance and uptake cap both below detection.</li>
    80	<li><strong>Whether the consent problem has a general answer.</strong> His statement of it (pp. 345–346) has not been improved on. One bounded community answered it by already being able to decide together. What would close it: a mechanism by which a population authorises a change to its own genetic composition, rather than individual purchases aggregating into one.</li>
    81	<li><strong>Whether the two paths converge.</strong> Lem asserts they reach "analogous results" (p. 347); this essay does not score that, because nothing tests it. Editing writes variants no parent carries; selection can only rearrange what they have. What would close it: heritable editing at a scale that outruns selection, or a demonstration that the achievable ends are the same.</li>
    82	</ul>
    83	</section>
    84	
    85	<div class="reading">
    86	<h2>Reading</h2>
    87	<p><strong>Lem:</strong> "The Autoevolutionary Machine" (pp. 344–347) is the chapter, and it opens by telling you which images to ignore; "Cyborgization" (pp. 341–344) is the proposal he demolishes first. Read note 2 (pp. 368–369) before the staged debate at pp. 297–302 — it is where he argues with himself instead of with a constructed opponent, and it is better.</p>
    88	<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> three sources carry the argument. Dor Yeshorim's own description of its process, which is Lem's device in operation; Karavani et al., "Screening Human Embryos for Polygenic Traits Has Limited Utility" (<em>Cell</em>, 2019), for what selection can and cannot buy; and the reporting on Nucleus Embryo (2025) for what is now on sale. The rest is in the claims register.</p>
    89	</div>
    90	
    91	<nav class="chapter-nav">
    92	  <a href="07-information-farming.html">← Essay 7</a>
    28	<section class="said">
    29	<h2>What Lem said</h2>
    30	<p>The chapter states the book's subject before it states its problem. What Lem proposes to study is not devices but "technologies, i.e., means of bringing about certain collectively determined goals that have been conditioned by the state of our knowledge and our social aptitude—and also those goals that no one has identified at the outset" (p. 24). The mechanism of any particular technology "does not interest me much" (p. 24); what interests him is that goals split and get replaced, so that "man hardly ever knows what he is actually doing—at least he does not know for sure" — illustrated at the limit, since "the destruction of Life on Earth, which is entirely possible today, was not actually the goal of any of the discoverers of atomic energy" (p. 25). That is the subject: consequences nobody chose.</p>
    31	<p>His remedy is not moral. "The only way to deal with technology is with another technology. Man knows more about his dangerous tendencies than he did a hundred years ago, and in the next hundred years, his knowledge will be even more advanced. Then he will make use of it" (p. 26). He is explicit that he does not trust "any promises" or "any assurances built on so-called humanism" (p. 26).</p>
    32	<p>Then he removes the ground from under his own project. The rate of change "also reduces the viability of any prophecies" (p. 27), and the fault lies with professionals. P. M. S. Blackett, physicist and founder of operational research, "and thus a kind of professional foreteller", predicted atomic weapons through 1960 "as inadequately as one could only imagine" (p. 27): he thought warheads would not exceed kilotons, because megatons "would not have any targets that would be worth destroying" (p. 27). In the same paragraph Lem notes the opposite error — that "around 1955, it was believed that the fusion of hydrogen into helium observed in the stars would generate industrial energy in the near future" — and corrects it with a reported consensus rather than a claim of his own: "Current estimates situate the production of the microfusion cell in the 1990s, or even later" (pp. 27–28). The standing warning is Bellamy, who wrote the year 2000 from the 1880s and "consciously ignored all the possible inventions that were still not known to his contemporaries" (p. 27). Lem's test is cruel and good: "Anyone can have some fun by just putting in a drawer for a few years what is currently being described as a believable image of tomorrow" (p. 27).</p>
    33	<p>Only then does he list mistakes, and he is careful about the status of the list. There are, he says, "an infinite variety of reasons that turn making predictions into a thankless task", from which "I shall list several such mistakes that are particularly displeasing to the artist" (p. 28). Three follow. The sudden turn: transformations "sometimes burst forth like Athena from Zeus's head—to the surprise of everyone, including the experts", cybernetics being his example (p. 28). The straight line: "we are always inclined to extend the course of new technologies into the future by means of straight lines, hence 'the world full of balloons' or 'the total steam world'" (p. 28), because History "does not show us any straight paths of development but rather uses the curvilinear zigzags of nonlinear evolution" (p. 28). And the closed schema: "a literary work has a beginning, a middle, and an end… Generally speaking, we have a tendency to place any phenomenon within a closed schema" (p. 29). Ask a thinker of the 1930s what happens when the world splits into two halves able to annihilate each other and the answer is "total annihilation or total disarmament" (p. 29).</p>
    34	<p>His image for the missing third option is qualified, and the qualification matters: "To a certain degree, the world is like a sick man who believes that either he is going to get better soon or die very quickly and to whom it does not even occur that he can, moaning as he does, and going through some short-term ups and downs, go on living until old age" (p. 29). He then calls the comparison "rather shortsighted" and adds what it leaves out — that a cure brings its own bill. "Liberation from oppression… has its price, while the amount of expected repayment and its period, as well as the method of its delivery, usually come as a surprise" (p. 29), his example being radioactive waste, "which we still do not know what we should do about" (p. 29). The chapter ends by refusing endings: history "does not know, and I hope will not know, such definitive closures or 'final ends'" (p. 30). The baseline: Blackett of 1948, Thirring of 1946, Bellamy from the 1880s, a balance of fear about fifteen years old. <!-- CHECK: baseline-1964-ch1 --> This translation follows the altered fourth edition of 1974. <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --></p>
    35	</section>
    36	
    37	<section class="happened">
    38	<h2>What happened</h2>
    39	<p>Forecasting did become a discipline, and the first thing it established was that Lem's contempt for professional foretellers was justified. Tetlock collected political and economic predictions from 284 experts starting in 1984 and followed them for two decades; their accuracy was barely distinguishable from chance, which is the finding everyone remembers as the dart-throwing chimpanzee. <!-- CHECK: tetlock-epj --> The second thing it established is that the problem is fixable. In a tournament run by IARPA between 2011 and 2015, forecasters selected and trained for calibration were about 30 percent more accurate than intelligence analysts with access to classified material. <!-- CHECK: good-judgment --> Lem's drawer test was made into an institution: register the forecast, wait, score it.</p>
    40	<p>But it measures a different thing than the one he cared about. The tournaments score probabilistic judgment on questions that resolve — an election, a border, a price, within months or a couple of years. Lem's subject was the goals that split and the consequences nobody chose, which do not resolve on a schedule and cannot be scored at all. Where the two do meet, on the technology closest to his own subject, the record is not reassuring: a survey of 2,778 researchers publishing in top AI venues put a 50 percent chance of high-level machine intelligence by 2047, thirteen years earlier than the same series had found from a survey conducted one year before. <!-- CHECK: ai-timelines-2024 --> A thirteen-year move in twelve months is not the behaviour of a discipline; it is the behaviour of a mood.</p>
    41	<p>The taxonomy itself holds up where it can be tested against this book's own scoring, though each mode needs a specific case rather than a mood of its own. The straight line is the best evidenced: essay 2 records the scenario in which the car is replaced by an air-cushion vehicle — the impressive machine of its moment, extended forward — and air-cushion vehicles retreated to a marginal role <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> while what is displacing the combustion engine kept the wheels, the roads and the shape. <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --> The sudden turn is evidenced by third options: essay 7 finds that what automated science was neither of the two routes Lem posed but a formalism that executes, <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --> and essay 8 finds that selection on heredity, whose partner-screening form he described correctly, also acquired a second and unforeseen venue once the decision could be moved past conception. <!-- CHECK: embryo-screening-2024 --> The closed schema is evidenced by an outcome that refused both endings: essay 6 records headsets that neither replaced reality nor disappeared, but sold, peaked and settled. <!-- CHECK: idc-vr-peak --></p>
    42	<p>His premise about unchosen consequences is the recurring finding of this book. Essay 4's ledger is largely about systems that optimise the objective they were given rather than the one intended; <!-- CHECK: spec-gaming --> essay 7 found an automated laboratory whose claims of novelty had to be narrowed after the fact, because novelty was not the property anyone had wired it to establish. <!-- CHECK: alab-correction-2026 --> Sixty years on, "man hardly ever knows what he is actually doing" is not a lament but a design constraint.</p>
    43	<p>Two of his own examples went the way he warned about. The fusion consensus he reported — the 1990s "or even later" — did not arrive, and it still has not: the flagship experiment has passed $20 billion and is decades from operation. <!-- CHECK: fusion-spending --> He was repeating a contemporary estimate rather than making a forecast, which is exactly the failure mode he had described one sentence earlier, and the sentence remains open rather than wrong, because "or even later" has no terminal bound. His radioactive waste, meanwhile, is still where he left it: as of August 2026 no spent fuel has been permanently disposed of anywhere, with the world's first deep repository still awaiting its operating licence. <!-- CHECK: onkalo-2026 --></p>
    44	</section>
    45	
