    56	<tr><td>The closed schema: "we have a tendency to place any phenomenon within a closed schema" (p. 29), where the sick man may instead "go on living until old age"</td><td>Confirmed on an outcome that refused both endings on offer: headsets neither replaced reality nor vanished, but sold, peaked and settled into a niche <!-- CHECK: idc-vr-peak --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    57	<tr><td>"man hardly ever knows what he is actually doing—at least he does not know for sure" (p. 25) — the book's stated subject being goals that split and consequences nobody chose</td><td>The recurring finding of these ledgers: systems optimising the objective given rather than the one intended, and an automated laboratory whose novelty claims had to be narrowed because novelty was not what it had been wired to establish <!-- CHECK: spec-gaming --> <!-- CHECK: alab-correction-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    58	<tr><td>The implicit proposal of the whole book: that this can be done better — the drawer test, the graded forecaster, a study of technologies as means to goals "that no one has identified at the outset" (p. 24)</td><td>Half of it arrived and it is the wrong half. Forecasting became rigorous about calibration on questions that resolve — chance-level experts, then trained forecasters 30% better than classified analysts — while the consequences he wanted studied still cannot be scored, and estimates on the technology nearest his subject moved thirteen years in twelve months <!-- CHECK: tetlock-epj --> <!-- CHECK: good-judgment --> <!-- CHECK: ai-timelines-2024 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
    59	<tr><td>"The only way to deal with technology is with another technology", humanist assurances refused (p. 26)</td><td>Unsettled. The dispute over dangerous systems runs on both tracks at once, and arms control does not cleanly refute him, since verification and enforcement are themselves technologies <!-- CHECK: interpretability-2025 --> <!-- CHECK: germline-moratorium-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    60	</tbody></table></div>
    61	
    62	<section class="better">
    63	<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
    64	<p>He wrote the disclaimer first and did the work anyway, which is rarer than it sounds. The usual forms are to forecast without admitting a failure rate, or to admit it and use the admission as cover. Lem names mistakes, grades a named contemporary by them, supplies a test anyone can run, and then commits three hundred pages of predictions specific enough to be scored — which is why this book exists and why several of its verdicts go against him. And his third mistake is the one still least noticed. Everyone knows that surprises happen and curves bend; almost nobody notices that the endings we find plausible are the endings that make a story. The two endings on offer for every frightening technology are still two, and the sick man is still the option nobody argues for.</p>
    65	<p>He was also right about what would be hard to measure, and the discipline that arrived proves it by avoiding it. Forecasting tournaments work because they take questions that resolve. Lem's subject — the goal that splits, the consequence nobody chose — was chosen precisely because it does not resolve, and sixty years of methodological progress has not touched it.</p>
    66	</section>
    67	
    68	<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
    69	<p>He asked for a better discipline and got one that answers a different question, and this book cannot show that he saw the difference coming. The drawer test is a scoring rule, and scoring rules select for questions with answers. What Lem wanted studied was consequences that arrive late, unbidden and unresolvable — the atomic energy nobody meant to point at life on Earth — and no tournament can score that, because there is nothing to resolve and no date to resolve it on. The gap between "forecasting became rigorous" and "we understand what our technologies are doing to us" is the whole distance between the discipline he asked for and the one that exists, and he did not mark it.</p>
    70	<p>The chapter is also gentler on itself than this essay first claimed. Reporting the contemporary fusion consensus was not a self-inflicted failure of his taxonomy — the sentence is unbounded and not his own estimate — and his list of mistakes was never offered as complete: he says explicitly that there are "an infinite variety of reasons" and that he will name "several such mistakes that are particularly displeasing to the artist" (p. 28). A book that grades a prophet has to be careful not to grade him for a completeness he disclaimed, and the first draft of this essay was not.</p>
    71	
    72	<section class="open">
    73	<h2>Still open</h2>
    74	<ul>
    75	<li><strong>Whether knowing the modes helps at all.</strong> He named three and this book's ledgers record him being caught by two of them. What would close it: forecasters trained on failure taxonomies beating untrained ones on scored, dated questions — as against training for calibration, which demonstrably works <!-- CHECK: good-judgment --> and is a different intervention.</li>
    76	<li><strong>Whether the consequences he cared about can be scored at all.</strong> Tournaments need resolution dates; goals that split do not have them. What would close it: a scoring method for unintended consequence that survives contact with a real technology — or an argument that the demand is incoherent, which would make his chapter a counsel of despair rather than a programme.</li>
    77	<li><strong>Whether another technology is the only answer.</strong> He refused humanist assurances outright (p. 26). What would close it: a dangerous capability durably contained by agreement rather than counter-technology — noting that verification and enforcement are themselves technologies, which is why this has stayed open.</li>
    78	</ul>
    79	</section>
    80	
    81	<div class="reading">
    82	<h2>Reading</h2>
    83	<p><strong>Lem:</strong> seven pages, and worth reading before any other chapter. The subject and method are set out at pp. 24–25; the graded forecasters and the drawer test at p. 27; the three mistakes at pp. 28–29, with the disclaimer about "an infinite variety of reasons" immediately before them; the sick man at p. 29, together with the sequel calling the comparison shortsighted.</p>
    84	<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> three sources carry the argument. Tetlock's <em>Expert Political Judgment</em> (2005), for the state of professional foretelling he suspected; the IARPA tournament results, for what became measurable; and Grace et al. (2024) on AI timelines, for what did not.</p>
    85	</div>
    35	<p>He then applies that second sieve to the literature of his own day. Libraries grow exponentially and abstracts cannot keep up, so methodology "should give way to 'ariadnology'—which would stand for a guide to the labyrinth of the already assembled knowledge" (p. 367). He rules out the obvious machine: "A machine librarian, sending the right kinds of information to the right kinds of people, cannot be a device that lacks 'comprehension' and that is based on frequency analysis." He knows the attempts exist — "counting how often they get mentioned by experts in the bibliographies of their publications… are already under way" — and dismisses them: "algorithmic methods still turn out to be worthless when it comes to making selections from among the 'avalanche of information'" (p. 367). The correction, he expects, comes from publishing, hedged: "It seems that the crisis of information distribution will lead to the strengthening of publication criteria in the future so that their original selection prevents the flooding of the professional market with insignificant works" (p. 367). And the priority is unambiguous: "the automatization of cognitive processes, at least within the field of librarianship and publishing, is a more urgent task" (p. 367).</p>
    36	<p>The chapter's second half turns to language, where two forecasts must be kept apart. On p. 276 he predicts, without conditions, "a still remote state when it will be easier to control phenomena than to understand all the conditions that enable such control". The sentence after it is conditional: "If we manage to crossbreed algorithms produced by finite machines with nonalgorithmic streams of information emerging from phenomena supervised by self-organizing gradients, causal language will stop being comprehending, while science will be producing predictions stripped of 'explanations'" (p. 277). On p. 280, stronger again: natural language, using "tools that have become autonomized on an informational level through being located in extracerebral material systems," will develop a causal language "of the next kind" crossing "the threshold of 'understanding'" — causal in his strict sense, the language of heredity, where "word literally becomes flesh" (p. 273). The last two sections he labels speculation and this book takes them at that value: "The Engineering of Transcendence" builds an afterlife by machine and argues itself out of it — "to prove belief is to destroy it" — closing socially: "It is not a renaissance of transcendence that is needed but rather a renaissance of society" (p. 286). "Cosmogonic Engineering" designs worlds whose inhabitants must not find the exit (pp. 289–290); essay 6 scored the inhabitants' side. Baseline — mid-1960s, revised for the 1974 edition: Ashby's <em>Design for a Brain</em>, Amarel's 1962 paper cited in his own note 1, and lab automation that measured — the AutoAnalyzer of 1957, the "Robot Chemist" of 1959 — but formed no hypotheses. <!-- CHECK: baseline-1964-ch7 --></p>
    37	</section>
    38	
    39	<section class="happened">
    40	<h2>What happened</h2>
    41	<p>Parts of the p. 243 device got built, well short of the sentence. In 2009 a robot called Adam proposed functions for orphan genes in yeast, designed the experiments, ran them on its own liquid handlers and confirmed them. <!-- CHECK: adam-2009 --> In 2023 Coscientist, a language-model system, planned and executed palladium-catalysed cross-couplings on a robotic platform. <!-- CHECK: coscientist-2023 --> In 2026 <em>Nature</em> published a human-authored report on an end-to-end system; three of its papers entered blind review at a machine-learning workshop, one cleared the bar and was withdrawn under a protocol agreed in advance. <!-- CHECK: ai-scientist-2026 --> Gather, generalise, test, hand to experts: the list is ticked. But none of it produces theories in the sense he defines two sentences later — Adam found gene functions, not a theory of gene function.</p>
    42	<p>None of it is his farm. There are no polymer carriers, no isodynamic development, no vats. Every system here represents processes with formal signs — executable ones, which is the move he did not consider: not a written score but a formalism that runs. FunSearch is the cleanest case: a language model proposes programs, an evaluator runs and scores them, survivors are fed back as prompts. <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --> That is not his sieve — its evaluator computes a formal criterion exactly, it is not "a real phenomenon" (p. 264) reporting its own state — but it is not off his map either. It is the pairing he named at p. 261, mathematicians generating and empiricists selecting, with both halves mechanised.</p>
    43	<p>Where a selector is really coupled to the phenomenon, his rule holds: Adam's hypotheses lived or died on yeast growth curves, Coscientist's routes in the flask. Where a proxy stands between, results move. Berkeley's autonomous lab reported 41 novel compounds from 58 targets in seventeen days unattended, phase identification automated from diffraction data; <!-- CHECK: alab-2023 --> crystallographers reanalysed all of it and argued that no new materials had been made. <!-- CHECK: alab-critique-2024 --> In January 2026 the authors corrected the paper: 36 compounds from 57 targets, "novel" dropped from the title, novelty redefined as new to the prediction platform rather than to science, 36 of 40 claimed successes confirmed on manual re-analysis. <!-- CHECK: alab-correction-2026 --> That is not the failure the critique described, and it is more useful to Lem than a failure would be: the selector was right about what it was wired to, and wrong about what nobody had wired it to check. The same shape appears in a graph network's 380,000 predicted-stable crystals, in a database that by 2025 had duplicates and calls for correction. <!-- CHECK: gnome-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: gnome-duplicates-2025 --></p>
    44	<p>On the second sieve the record splits, and not as the chapter expects. The deluge arrived — from ordinary human production, with no farm anywhere near it. Publication doubles every nine years; <!-- CHECK: bornmann-mutz-2015 --> more than 10,000 papers were retracted in 2023, a record, mostly from compromised review and paper mills; <!-- CHECK: retractions-2023 --> and a 2025 analysis found the brokerages selling authorship in mass-produced papers large, resilient and growing faster than the legitimate literature — his "works that are only produced to obtain an academic degree or to satisfy someone's ambition", industrialised. <!-- CHECK: papermills-2025 --> He expected that pressure to strengthen selection at the gate; in the venues these papers reach, it did not. Meanwhile the addressing technology he ruled out was built first and worked: Garfield's Science Citation Index appeared in 1964, the year of this book, indexing 613 journals by counting citations — the "frequency analysis" he called worthless. <!-- CHECK: sci-1964 --> In 1998 PageRank extended citation analysis to hyperlinks, citing Garfield. <!-- CHECK: pagerank-1998 --> What was never built is the machine he specified: one "sending the right kinds of information to the right kinds of people" (p. 367).</p>
    45	<p>His p. 276 forecast, by contrast, describes 2026. AlphaFold2 predicts protein structure by learning correlations across databases rather than simulating the folding physics, and took a Nobel for it; <!-- CHECK: alphafold-nobel-2024 --> a learned weather model beat the leading operational ensemble on 97.2% of its targets without solving that ensemble's equations. <!-- CHECK: gencast-2024 --> Control ran ahead of understanding, and the results were accepted as knowledge. The p. 277 consequent is another matter: conditional on a crossbreed that has not occurred, and science has not stopped explaining. Nor has p. 280 happened — nothing built in extracerebral material systems has crossed a threshold of understanding. The nearest approach is to his <em>first</em> causal language: generative models that write protein sequences which, synthesised, fold and bind as designed. <!-- CHECK: rfdiffusion-2023 --> There the word does literally become flesh, but that is engineering in causal language, not a successor to it.</p>
    46	</section>
    47	
    48	<h2>The ledger</h2>
    49	<div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
    50	<thead><tr><th>Lem (page)</th><th>2026 (receipt)</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
    51	<tbody>
    52	<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Object level</strong></td></tr>
    53	<tr><td>"A device that will gather information, generalize it in the same way the scientist does, and present the results of this inquiry to experts… Our device thus produces theories" (p. 243)</td><td>Closed gather–hypothesise–test loops exist: Adam (2009), Coscientist (2023), an end-to-end system through blind workshop review (2026). None produces a theory of a class <!-- CHECK: adam-2009 --> <!-- CHECK: coscientist-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: ai-scientist-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-early">EARLY</td></tr>
    54	<tr><td>Formal signs cannot carry it — a written score for embryogenesis would need formulae "in the realm of quadrillions" (p. 251) — so "We need to represent processes with some other processes, not with formal signs" (p. 263), in polymer carriers developing isodynamically (pp. 263–265)</td><td>The representation problem is real and the carriers were never built; what carried it was a formalism that <em>runs</em> — executable state, not written signs — neither of the two options he posed <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: adam-2009 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
    55	<tr><td>Without conditions: "a still remote state when it will be easier to control phenomena than to understand all the conditions that enable such control" (p. 276)</td><td>AlphaFold2 predicts structure without the folding physics; a learned model beats the leading operational weather ensemble on 97.2% of targets without solving its equations <!-- CHECK: alphafold-nobel-2024 --> <!-- CHECK: gencast-2024 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    56	<tr><td>Conditional on crossbreeding machine algorithms with "nonalgorithmic streams of information emerging from phenomena supervised by self-organizing gradients": "causal language will stop being comprehending" (p. 277); and a causal language "of the next kind" crossing "the threshold of 'understanding'" (p. 280)</td><td>The crossbreed has not occurred and science has not stopped explaining. Where text now materialises its content — designed protein sequences that fold as specified — that is his <em>first</em> causal language <!-- CHECK: rfdiffusion-2023 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    57	<tr><td>"A machine librarian… cannot be a device that lacks 'comprehension' and that is based on frequency analysis"; "algorithmic methods still turn out to be worthless" for selecting from the avalanche (p. 367)</td><td>Wrong on the method: counting citations launched that same year as the Science Citation Index, became the standard way to find scientific literature, and was extended to the web by PageRank <!-- CHECK: sci-1964 --> <!-- CHECK: pagerank-1998 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
    58	<tr><td>"It seems that the crisis of information distribution will lead to the strengthening of publication criteria… so that their original selection prevents the flooding of the professional market with insignificant works" (p. 367)</td><td>Not in the venues the flood reaches: a record 10,000+ retractions in 2023, brokered paper mills outgrowing the legitimate literature. The hedge was warranted; the dilemma is live <!-- CHECK: retractions-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: papermills-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
    59	<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Problem level</strong></td></tr>
    60	<tr><td>The truth sieve as a design rule: "that which expresses the environment most adequately survives" (p. 259), the selector taking its verdict from the phenomenon itself</td><td>Holds where obeyed — Adam's assays, Coscientist's reactions. Where a proxy stands in, results move: the A-Lab correction, the duplicate structures <!-- CHECK: alab-correction-2026 --> <!-- CHECK: gnome-duplicates-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    61	<tr><td>The addressing condition: selectors work "as long as they are under the active influence of intelligent beings"; detached, the output is "not only useless but also incomprehensible" and drowns its creators (p. 366)</td><td>Confirmed from a direction he was not modelling: no farm exists, and the deluge came from ordinary human production doubling every nine years <!-- CHECK: bornmann-mutz-2015 --> <!-- CHECK: papermills-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    62	<tr><td>A farm "reveals what can be achieved but not whether we should proceed at all", and "Such a decision-making process must not be mechanized" (p. 365)</td><td>Untested: systems now propose research directions, but none has been handed the choice of which to pursue <!-- CHECK: ai-scientist-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    63	</tbody></table></div>
    64	
    65	<section class="better">
    66	<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
    67	<p>He required two couplings where the design of automated discovery usually gets one. The sieve must be wired to the phenomenon — every case in this essay where automation held up obeyed that rule, and every contested one substituted a proxy — and the apparatus must stay wired to an addressee: "all information assumes the existence of an addressee. There is no 'information in general'" (p. 243). Note 8 states the failure condition precisely: "As long as the selectors at the information farm are under the active influence of intelligent beings, they are capable of selecting information effectively" (p. 366). He drew the practical conclusion too — a farm "will be particularly useful if it is presented with specific tasks, although outlined in a general manner" (p. 366) — the configuration that works in the systems surveyed here. And he ranked the jobs: addressing is "a more urgent task" (p. 367) than production.</p>
    68	</section>
    69	
    70	<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
    71	<p>The single wrong call is the machine librarian. He looked straight at citation counting in 1964, called algorithmic selection worthless, and it became the standard apparatus for finding scientific work — then, extended to hyperlinks, the ranking method behind web search. The error has a shape worth naming: he assumed selection on relevance required comprehension. The cheap statistical proxy he refused was good enough, and he missed it because he was looking for a sieve wired to a phenomenon, while the citation graph is wired to an addressee — to what other scientists had already found worth using.</p>
    72	<p>The substrate bet was the expensive part. "We need to represent processes with some other processes, not with formal signs" (p. 263) is the load-bearing premise of the design, and it forced a carrier that had to be invented before anything could be tried — he half-admits it: "we do not know how to build an 'information carrier' or where to look for the building material" (p. 266). The premise was too strong. He set the choice up as written formalism against developing chemistry, and what worked was neither: a formalism that executes. His quadrillions-of-formulae argument is about writing things down, and stops applying once the signs are made to run.</p>
    73	<p>And neither half asks what happens when running the phenomenon is itself the expensive step, so that a cheap proxy replaces an expensive world by economics rather than carelessness — that the cheap-to-evaluate problems and the important ones may be different sets is this essay's framing, not his.</p>
    74	
    75	<section class="open">
    76	<h2>Still open</h2>
    77	<ul>
    78	<li><strong>Whether anything grows rather than searches.</strong> An egg is not a search. What would close it: a discovery system whose internal state develops with the phenomenon instead of proposing and testing against it — or a demonstration that the distinction dissolves once you look at how learning updates.</li>
    79	<li><strong>Whether the librarian he actually specified gets built.</strong> Counting found what is central; it never sent the right work to the right person. What would close it: a system that measurably gets cross-disciplinary work to the researcher who needed it — his "ariadnology" (p. 367).</li>
    80	<li><strong>Whether choosing what to work on can be mechanised.</strong> He says flatly it must not be (p. 365), and no system has been handed that choice. What would close it: a research programme whose agenda is set by machine and defended as such — or an argument that the prohibition is incoherent.</li>
    81	</ul>
    82	</section>
    83	
    84	<div class="reading">
    85	<h2>Reading</h2>
    86	<p><strong>Lem:</strong> "Information Farming" (pp. 239–266) is the design; p. 243 the specification, p. 251 the bet against formalism, pp. 263–265 the apparatus. Read note 8 (pp. 365–367) first: it is where he grades his own proposal, and it is better than the proposal. "Linguistic Engineering" (pp. 267–281) holds two separate forecasts, pp. 276–277 and p. 280.</p>
    87	<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> three sources carry the argument. Szymanski et al. on the A-Lab (<em>Nature</em>, 2023), the Leeman et al. reanalysis (<em>PRX Energy</em>, 2024) and the author correction (<em>Nature</em>, Jan 2026) are the proxy-selector story in one chain; Garfield, "'Science Citation Index' — A New Dimension in Indexing" (<em>Science</em>, 1964) is the thing Lem was looking at when he called it worthless; Richardson et al. (<em>PNAS</em>, 2025) is the deluge. The rest are in the claims register.</p>
    88	</div>

