    58	<tr><td>The implicit proposal of the whole book: that this can be done better — the drawer test, the graded forecaster, a study of technologies as means to goals "that no one has identified at the outset" (p. 24)</td><td>Half of it arrived and it is the wrong half. Forecasting became rigorous about calibration on questions that resolve — chance-level experts, then trained forecasters 30% better than classified analysts — while the consequences he wanted studied still cannot be scored, and estimates on the technology nearest his subject moved thirteen years in twelve months <!-- CHECK: tetlock-epj --> <!-- CHECK: good-judgment --> <!-- CHECK: ai-timelines-2024 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
    59	<tr><td>"The only way to deal with technology is with another technology", humanist assurances refused (p. 26)</td><td>Unsettled. The dispute over dangerous systems runs on both tracks at once, and arms control does not cleanly refute him, since verification and enforcement are themselves technologies <!-- CHECK: interpretability-2025 --> <!-- CHECK: germline-moratorium-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    60	</tbody></table></div>
    61	
    62	<section class="better">
    63	<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
    64	<p>He wrote the disclaimer first and did the work anyway, which is rarer than it sounds. The usual forms are to forecast without admitting a failure rate, or to admit it and use the admission as cover. Lem names mistakes, grades a named contemporary by them, supplies a test anyone can run, and then commits three hundred pages of predictions specific enough to be scored — which is why this book exists and why several of its verdicts go against him. And his third mistake is the one still least noticed. Everyone knows that surprises happen and curves bend; almost nobody notices that the endings we find plausible are the endings that make a story. The two endings on offer for every frightening technology are still two, and the sick man is still the option nobody argues for.</p>
    65	<p>He was also right about what would be hard to measure, and the discipline that arrived proves it by avoiding it. Forecasting tournaments work because they take questions that resolve. Lem's subject — the goal that splits, the consequence nobody chose — was chosen precisely because it does not resolve, and sixty years of methodological progress has not touched it.</p>
    66	</section>
    67	
    68	<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
    69	<p>He asked for a better discipline and got one that answers a different question, and this book cannot show that he saw the difference coming. The drawer test is a scoring rule, and scoring rules select for questions with answers. What Lem wanted studied was consequences that arrive late, unbidden and unresolvable — the atomic energy nobody meant to point at life on Earth — and no tournament can score that, because there is nothing to resolve and no date to resolve it on. The gap between "forecasting became rigorous" and "we understand what our technologies are doing to us" is the whole distance between the discipline he asked for and the one that exists, and he did not mark it.</p>
    70	<p>The chapter is also gentler on itself than this essay first claimed. Reporting the contemporary fusion consensus was not a self-inflicted failure of his taxonomy — the sentence is unbounded and not his own estimate — and his list of mistakes was never offered as complete: he says explicitly that there are "an infinite variety of reasons" and that he will name "several such mistakes that are particularly displeasing to the artist" (p. 28). A book that grades a prophet has to be careful not to grade him for a completeness he disclaimed, and the first draft of this essay was not.</p>
    71	
    72	<section class="open">
    73	<h2>Still open</h2>
    74	<ul>
    75	<li><strong>Whether knowing the modes helps at all.</strong> He named three and this book's ledgers record him being caught by two of them. What would close it: forecasters trained on failure taxonomies beating untrained ones on scored, dated questions — as against training for calibration, which demonstrably works <!-- CHECK: good-judgment --> and is a different intervention.</li>
    76	<li><strong>Whether the consequences he cared about can be scored at all.</strong> Tournaments need resolution dates; goals that split do not have them. What would close it: a scoring method for unintended consequence that survives contact with a real technology — or an argument that the demand is incoherent, which would make his chapter a counsel of despair rather than a programme.</li>
    42	<p>The scenario's successor never arrived. Air-cushion vehicles did not take the roads, and at sea they retreated: the car-carrying cross-Channel service closed in October 2000, beaten by a tunnel, and large commercial hovercraft effectively disappeared, leaving one short scheduled route. <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> Wheel drive is untouched. What is displacing the internal combustion engine is a different motor under the same wheels: electric cars reached a quarter of global new-car sales in 2025, more than half in China, so at least three-quarters of new cars still burn fuel. <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --></p>
    43	<p>The relic half is being decided by something outside the model. Lem's small petrol engine is supposed to outlive the car in a lateral branch. Instead California required most newly manufactured small off-road engines — petrol engines at or below 25 horsepower, which covers lawnmowers — to be zero-emission from January 2024, while existing machines stay legal to own and resell. <!-- CHECK: sore-ban-2024 --> The relic is not being outcompeted; the niche is being closed to new entrants by regulation. Lem is clear that technical evolution is "not… people-free" and, unlike bioevolution, not amoral (p. 38) — but the chapter models human agency as choice about direction, not as a body that can prohibit a fit technology and protect an unfit one.</p>
    44	<p>The same pressure runs the other way too. Manufacturers began dropping AM radio from electric cars around 2023, because the high-voltage drivetrain interferes with reception — habitat loss, and the reverse of the diversification under television pressure Lem describes at p. 36. What followed has no biological counterpart: the older technology acquired a lobby. The AM Radio for Every Vehicle Act, reintroduced in January 2025 and advanced by the Senate Commerce Committee within a week, would compel manufacturers to carry AM reception from model year 2028. <!-- CHECK: am-radio-ev --> Species do not petition a legislature against their competitors.</p>
    45	<p>Where the analogy earns its keep is the mechanism, not the artifacts. The vertically launching aeroplane already existed when he wrote — the P.1127 hovered in 1960, the Harrier entered service in 1969 — so the forecast is not that it would appear. The testable part is his account of <em>why</em>: a lineage defending a niche rather than conquering one. The V-22 entered service in 2007 and the F-35B in 2015, and neither displaced the helicopter, which is what niche defence predicts and displacement does not. <!-- CHECK: vtol-aircraft --> The civil version, which he did not describe, remains uncertified. <!-- CHECK: evtol-2026 --></p>
    46	<p>The pattern holds where machines compete for a physical niche, and something else happens where they do not. Software relics do not retreat to the margins; they stay in the middle. COBOL, written for mainframes contemporary with this book, is still load-bearing in core banking, on industry estimates that put it somewhere between roughly 220 and 800 billion lines in production — figures that are trade estimates rather than audited counts. <!-- CHECK: cobol-production --> The direction is not in doubt even if the number is: this is not a lateral branch vegetating in a peripheral niche, it is the centre of the financial system sixty years after the environment that produced it disappeared. In software the cost of replacement falls on whoever switches, and nothing forces the switch, so a relic can occupy the core indefinitely.</p>
    47	<p>His p. 43 claim went the other way. When he wrote it, technology had "only been creating narrowly specialized devices"; the direction then reversed, and the systems that now matter most are valued for not being specialised. He named the route — universality via "self-organizing systems… capable of adaptive self-programming" — and what arrived is self-programmed in a loose sense, learned rather than written, but by gradient descent on large corpora rather than the theory he meant. <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --> His refusal at p. 56 to reserve creative work for people has held: what automated first was writing, drawing and coding. Programmers have not been dispensed with. <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --></p>
    48	<p>Note 6's mechanism — cross-fertilisation between technical domains, which biology cannot perform — is visible in the origin of that technology. The founding result of deep learning was trained on two consumer graphics cards built for rendering video games. <!-- CHECK: alexnet-2012 --> That is one instance of cross-domain recombination, offered as this essay's illustration rather than as proof of the note's larger claims about acceleration or unpredictability, which it cannot carry alone.</p>
    49	</section>
    50	
