    68	
    69	<section class="better">
    70	<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
    71	<p>He treated the analogy as a claim with consequences rather than a figure of speech, and he named mechanisms — niche defence, radiation into empty space, relics in lateral branches — instead of gesturing at resemblance. Sixty years of technology writing has since made "ecosystem", "DNA" and "evolution" into decoration, applied to firms and products with nothing at stake and nothing checkable. His version can be graded, which is why there is a MISS in this ledger at all. He was also better than most of his successors on two specific points. He refused, in 1966, the consolation that creative work is safe from automation, and refused it on the right ground: not that machines would soon write poems, but that nobody had any evidence for the exemption and no such evidence could exist. And in the same paragraph he kept capability apart from motive — "'cleverer' does not yet mean 'rebellious'" (p. 57) — which is precisely the inference most current argument about intelligent machines runs together.</p>
    72	</section>
    73	
    74	<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
    75	<p>He picked the wrong successor for the car, and picked it for a reason worth naming. The air-cushion vehicle was, when he wrote, the impressive machine: it did the thing that looked hard, abolishing the wheel. What actually displaced the combustion engine was a substitution so conservative it kept the wheels, the roads, the shape and the name. His own framework handles that better than his example did — evolution changes what is cheap to change, and a lineage under pressure does not redesign its body plan — but the framework is in the chapter and the hovercraft is in the prediction.</p>
    76	<p>The larger gap is a pressure the model does not contain. Lem is explicit that technical evolution is not people-free and not amoral, so this is not a chapter that thinks technology runs itself. But the agency he models is directional: societies choosing what to build and how fast. What decided the fate of the small petrol engine was neither competition nor choice of direction but a rule about what may be manufactured, and what is keeping AM radio alive in cars is a bill. A regulator can close a niche to a technology that is winning it and compel the carriage of one that is losing. Between "competition" and "human choice" there is a third thing — enforceable prohibition — and the two-evolutions frame has no slot for it, which is why the chapter's most concrete illustration is being settled in a way the chapter cannot describe.</p>
    77	
    78	<section class="open">
    79	<h2>Still open</h2>
    80	<ul>
    81	<li><strong>Whether the classical car leaves a relic at all.</strong> His pattern predicts one; regulation is closing the obvious candidate to new manufacture first, in one jurisdiction. What would close it: the combustion engine surviving in some protected niche after passenger cars have gone electric — or its disappearance everywhere at once, which would be an extinction with no biological analogue.</li>
    82	<li><strong>Whether software ever goes extinct.</strong> Sixty years in, code from this book's decade is still in the centre of the financial system. What would close it: a large legacy stack actually retired rather than wrapped — or an argument that extinction is the wrong category for something that costs nothing to keep running.</li>
    83	<li><strong>Whether cleverer becomes rebellious.</strong> He separated the two and said the intermediaries would be "in some sense 'cleverer' than their human designer" without that implying revolt (p. 57). What would close it: a capable system that pursues ends its designers did not give it and will not accept — or a demonstration that capability and motive are separable by construction.</li>
    84	</ul>
    43	<p>That also complicates his price. He asked for "a great amount of equipment" and possibly transmitters, and compared the bill to nuclear-energy research; what happened was a programme commitment of about $10 million a year riding on public infrastructure, against $790 million for US fusion in a single year and more than $20 billion sunk into ITER. <!-- CHECK: fusion-spending --> <!-- CHECK: breakthrough-listen-2015 --> Those are not like-for-like quantities, and nobody has assembled the all-in figure his sentence actually names. What can be said is that no state ever treated the search as a project of that size, and that the searching which did happen was cheap because it borrowed dishes built for something else.</p>
    44	<p>The transmission asymmetry is real and remains the field's live dispute, though the history is less tidy than "nobody transmits". Frank Drake broadcast the Arecibo message toward M13 in 1974, with considerable fanfare; <!-- CHECK: arecibo-1974 --> a Russian astronomer sent several messages from Evpatoria; and METI International was founded in 2015 to send more. What is true is that sustained deliberate transmission never became normal, and that in the same year a statement issued around the AAAS meeting held that any decision to transmit "must be based upon a worldwide consensus, and not a decision based upon the wishes of a few individuals with access to powerful communications equipment." <!-- CHECK: meti-2015 --> Everyone listens and almost nobody calls, which is Lem's economic trap with a moral argument now bolted onto it.</p>
    45	<p>His information argument is sound and its novelty is smaller than it looks: Ozma was already hunting artificial regularity against galactic noise when he wrote, as he says himself at p. 64. What the argument explains is why the method stayed that way. Radio SETI still concentrates on narrowband, repetitive signals, because natural astrophysical emission is broadband while an efficiently encoded transmission is statistically hard to distinguish from noise — so the detectable beacon is by construction the one somebody wasted bandwidth to make detectable. <!-- CHECK: narrowband-seti --> His stronger claim, that the conversations may already be in the archives as background, remains untestable and is a live reason the null result is weaker than it sounds.</p>
    46	<p>Star flight has not moved, but sixty years is not the several hundred he allowed. No propulsion has appeared that changes von Hoerner's arithmetic. The only funded interstellar programme abandoned the crewed ship entirely: Breakthrough Starshot, announced in 2016 with $100 million, proposes gram-scale laser-driven sails to a neighbouring star — a different journey from the intergalactic crossing von Hoerner costed, and a proof-of-concept programme rather than a mission. <!-- CHECK: starshot-2016 --> It is privately funded too.</p>
    47	</section>
    48	
