    52	<tbody>
    53	<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Object level</strong></td></tr>
    54	<tr><td>The search "requires some serious material investment… more substantial than the current investment in research on nuclear energy" (p. 90)</td><td>Not settled, because nobody built what he costed. The searching that happened ran at about $10M a year on borrowed public dishes, against $790M for US fusion in one year — not a like-for-like comparison, and the all-in figure his sentence names was never assembled <!-- CHECK: breakthrough-listen-2015 --> <!-- CHECK: bl-public-facilities --> <!-- CHECK: fusion-spending --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    55	<tr><td>Scientists must "cultivate a generation of rulers who will be inclined to dip deeply enough into the public coffers" (p. 90)</td><td>The bottleneck was where he put it and the remedy was not what happened. The one large public programme was killed by a Senate amendment about a year in; dedicated searching then ran on philanthropy — an array, $100M, a $200M bequest — while observing on publicly built telescopes <!-- CHECK: nasa-seti-1993 --> <!-- CHECK: ata-2011 --> <!-- CHECK: seti-bequest-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: bl-public-facilities --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
    56	<tr><td>Near-light travel needs annihilating roughly the mass of the Moon and is "likely to remain impossible over the next several hundred years" (p. 89, on von Hoerner's analysis)</td><td>Unchanged, but sixty years is not several hundred, so the forecast is not due. The only funded programme swapped the crewed intergalactic ship for gram-scale sails to a neighbouring star <!-- CHECK: starshot-2016 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    57	<tr><td>Hypothesis III: civilisations are frequent and long-lived, with only the exponential phase brief, "less than twenty thousand years" (p. 82); and note 3's selection of isolation as "the most probable" and "dominant hypothesis"</td><td>Untestable on present evidence. No extraterrestrial civilisation has been observed, so neither the phase length nor the isolation can be scored either way</td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    58	<tr><td>Von Hoerner's 65% chance of self-destruction is "too naïve", because a statistical rule admits outliers and "just a few civilizations with an 'abnormal' life span… would influence the whole Galaxy" (pp. 67, 83)</td><td>The objection stands as logic and is unresolved as fact: the Great Filter debate has not settled where the filter sits, and no observation distinguishes his outlier argument from universal extinction <!-- CHECK: seti-haystack-2018 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    59	<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Problem level</strong></td></tr>
    60	<tr><td>Contact is a problem of cost and incentive — who builds, who pays, who transmits — not only of physics and biology (pp. 89–90)</td><td>The shape of the field. Its history is a funding history: a programme cancelled by amendment, an array saved by tracking orbital debris, a search bought privately and run on public dishes, an endowment left in a will <!-- CHECK: ata-2011 --> <!-- CHECK: bl-public-facilities --> <!-- CHECK: seti-bequest-2023 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    61	<tr><td>"If, for economic reasons, all the civilizations were only working on reception, then no one would ever hear anyone else, as many have already pointed out" (p. 90) — credited by him to others</td><td>Holds as a recurring incentive problem, on the only case observable: Earth listens continuously and has transmitted deliberately a handful of times since Arecibo in 1974, with a 2015 statement demanding worldwide consensus before anyone transmits again <!-- CHECK: arecibo-1974 --> <!-- CHECK: meti-2015 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    62	<tr><td>An efficiently coded message "starts to resemble noise", so a civilisation wanting to be found must send simple, repeatable "call signs" (p. 90)</td><td>Correct, and it explains why the method he watched being born stayed put: searches still hunt narrowband repetitive beacons because natural emission is broadband and dense encoding is indistinguishable from noise. Ozma was already doing this in 1960, so the argument explains a practice rather than predicting one <!-- CHECK: narrowband-seti --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    63	<tr><td>"a complete absence of any positive data will be of even greater significance—which will increase even further the longer such attempts take and the more sensitive the instruments used in them are" (p. 80) — with "Today it is still too early for this"</td><td>Still too early, on his own terms and by a wide margin. The eight-dimensional accounting puts the fraction of the radio search space examined at roughly a drinking glass against Earth's oceans <!-- CHECK: seti-haystack-2018 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    64	</tbody></table></div>
    65	
    66	<section class="better">
    67	<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
    68	<p>He noticed that the question has a budget, and that the budget has a politics. Most writing about contact — most of it far more recent than this — treats the search as something that simply happens, as though telescopes pointed themselves, and treats the silence as data arriving free of charge. Lem asked who would pay, and then asked the harder version: if listening is cheaper than shouting, everyone will listen, and a universe of listeners is silent for reasons that have nothing to do with whether anyone is there. He also had the discipline to state the information problem in its strong form, that the better the message the more it looks like nothing, so the only thing findable is what somebody deliberately wasted effort to make findable. And in note 3 he did the thing this book most often finds him failing to do: he said which hypothesis he had chosen and why, in writing, where it could be held against him.</p>
    69	</section>
    70	
    71	<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
    72	<p>He costed the wrong object and framed the problem as scale. His estimate — more than nuclear-energy research — set contact up as something only a state could attempt, which made political will the binding constraint. What actually bound was neither money nor will but attention. The sums were small enough that a single donor could cover them and small enough that no government felt obliged to; a programme costing 0.1 percent of NASA's budget is not killed because it is expensive but because it is cheap enough to cut without argument and strange enough to make an easy vote. And the equipment problem dissolved in a way he did not foresee: you do not need to build the great amount of equipment if you can rent time on dishes built for radio astronomy.</p>
    73	<p>The second cost lands on the chapter's main argument, and by his own standard. Thirty pages treat the silence as something the theory of biogenesis must answer to, and he does say, carefully, "Today it is still too early for this" (p. 80). Sixty more years of listening have not changed that, because the listening covered a fraction of the search space best expressed in scientific notation. The silence may yet be the universe's answer. It is not yet evidence of anything, and the chapter's confidence that a sufficiently long null result would force a revision of biogenesis is a promissory note that has not begun to come due.</p>
    74	
    75	<section class="open">
    76	<h2>Still open</h2>
    77	<ul>
    78	<li><strong>What the null result is worth.</strong> The completeness accounting exists now and gives a number close to zero. What would close it: a search that covers enough of the space for the silence to constrain anything — or an argument that the space is too large in principle for any null result to constrain.</li>
    79	<li><strong>Whether anyone will transmit.</strong> His asymmetry predicts permanent mutual listening, and the record since Arecibo is consistent with it. What would close it: a sustained transmission programme with international sanction — or a formal prohibition, which would confirm the trap rather than escape it.</li>
    80	<li><strong>Whether isolation is why.</strong> Note 3 selects it as his dominant hypothesis and nothing since bears on it. What would close it: any technosignature search sensitive to a civilisation that is not expanding — or a demonstration that a civilisation which stops growing necessarily stops being detectable, which would make the hypothesis unfalsifiable and settle it the other way.</li>
    81	</ul>
    82	</section>
    83	
    84	<div class="reading">
    85	<h2>Reading</h2>
    86	<p><strong>Lem:</strong> "Future Prospects" (pp. 87–92) carries this essay, and within it the two pages from p. 89. But read note 3 (pp. 358–359) first: it is where he says which hypothesis he actually chose, and the chapter never does. "Votum Separatum" (pp. 83–86) is the alternative he sketches to anthropomorphic search.</p>
    87	<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> three sources carry the argument. Wright, Kanodia and Lubar on how much of the haystack has been searched (<em>AJ</em>, 2018), which reframes everything; the record of NASA's High Resolution Microwave Survey and its cancellation, for what happened to public funding; and the 2015 statement on messaging extraterrestrial intelligence, for why almost nobody transmits.</p>
    88	</div>
    44	<p>The belief material came back in a form he would have enjoyed, and it is also not his claim. His homeostat believes because refusing to act is not available to a living thing. In 2025 OpenAI published an account of hallucination that arrives somewhere formally similar by another road: models are graded by tests where abstention scores zero and a guess sometimes scores one. <!-- CHECK: openai-hallucination-2025 --> Lem's regulator believes on pain of death; ours because we mark it like a student. The mechanisms differ and the ledger does not score them together. His actual experiment was never run. The nearest thing is an argument: a 2024 paper that AI welfare should be taken seriously, and a lab opening a model-welfare programme in April 2025. <!-- CHECK: ai-welfare-2024 --> <!-- CHECK: model-welfare-2025 --> He expected the demand for rights from the machine; so far it comes from us.</p>
    45	</section>
    46	
    47	<h2>The ledger</h2>
    48	<div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
    49	<thead><tr><th>Lem (page)</th><th>2026 (receipt)</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
    50	<tbody>
    51	<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Object level</strong></td></tr>
    52	<tr><td>Ordering: controlling evolution, changing regenerative potential and "orchestrat[ing] genetic traits in fetuses" will all be possible "long before… we gain the ability to create the chromosome apparatus of a nucleus in an artificial manner" (p. 193)</td><td>Neither side has happened, so the order cannot be scored. All sixteen yeast chromosomes are individually synthesised and validated, but consolidating them into one organism is still the project's final phase; no trait is orchestrated in a fetus <!-- CHECK: sc2-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: embryo-screening-2024 --> <!-- CHECK: germline-moratorium-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    53	<tr><td>Chromosomes written down "on a piece of paper, in the symbolic language of chemistry", synthesised, and the resulting "laboratory egg" sent into development (p. 192)</td><td>A bacterial genome was assembled from digitised sequence and transplanted into a recipient cell that then replicated under it (2010) — a precursor, not a path: no synthetic eukaryotic nucleus, no egg, no embryogenesis, and no dated route from one to the other <!-- CHECK: synthetic-genome-2010 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    54	<tr><td>An "artificial brain, created from natural tissue", grown by starting an egg and pruning the embryo (p. 191)</td><td>No attempt found in the literature surveyed here. Cultured neurons trained in closed loop are a different object with a different history <!-- CHECK: dishbrain-2022 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    55	<tr><td>"Believing machines": homeostats with unprogrammed brains in varied bodies and worlds, run to see what metaphysics each grows (pp. 138–140)</td><td>Not run, so far as this essay could establish. What is studied instead is what trained models inherit from human text <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    56	<tr><td>Such a machine "will demand equal rights with the practitioners of the religion with which it will identify" (p. 140)</td><td>No machine has. The moral-status argument is live and institutional, and it is made by people about machines <!-- CHECK: ai-welfare-2024 --> <!-- CHECK: model-welfare-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    57	<tr><td>Mathematics stops being a warehouse and becomes "a phantomological generator, a creator of worlds" (p. 184)</td><td>Not among the work surveyed here; the traffic still runs the other way, from empirical need to structures already on the shelf <!-- CHECK: alphafold-nobel-2024 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    58	<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Problem level</strong></td></tr>
    59	<tr><td>What the designer gives up: not the bricks. "The designer is not a narrow pragmatist, like a builder who is constructing his house from bricks, uninterested in where these bricks came from and what they are… The designer knows everything about his bricks" (p. 176) — he drops the definitive question and keeps the materials science</td><td>2026 dropped both. The stated method is to build with less understanding, not more <!-- CHECK: bitter-lesson-2019 -->; the textbook account of why the material generalises was falsified and not replaced <!-- CHECK: zhang-2017 -->; and the builder says outright that it does not understand what it ships <!-- CHECK: interpretability-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
    60	<tr><td>Where "the surplus" in a theory's predictions comes from. His own answer: "there exists a continuity of transformations in the world… their feedback" (p. 188). What he cannot settle is the accounting — x bits in, x + n out, "a true informational perpetuum mobile!" that "cannot be resolved on the basis of current information theory" (pp. 188–189)</td><td>The accounting is still not done in his terms; no modern result frames itself as structural-information balance <!-- CHECK: alphazero-2018 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    61	</tbody></table></div>
    62	
    63	<section class="better">
    64	<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
    65	<p>He knew it was a bargain and priced both halves. "The Silence of the Designer" is a list of things given up — the definitive question, the demand that a model resemble its object, the hope that usefulness and truth are one word — and a list of things kept, starting with the bricks. He calls the posture "active silence" (p. 176): not having nothing to say, but deciding which sentences are not worth finishing. What the bitter lesson states as a lesson, Lem states as a choice with a price attached. Lem also says what the silence is <em>for</em>, which the bitter lesson does not: the Designer drops questions because he is trying to change something — "we only want to get to know the world to the extent that we can improve on it" (p. 176). Strip that clause and the method has no stopping rule at all.</p>
    66	</section>
    67	
    68	<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
    69	<p>He was wrong about what a designer keeps. The whole force of "The Silence of the Designer" is that giving up the definitive question is not the same as giving up knowledge of the material: his builder "knows everything about his bricks", and drops only what no experiment could settle. Sixty years on, the field that most resembles his Designer took the first half of that bargain and not the second. The stated method is to build with less understanding, not more. The textbook account of why the material works was falsified in 2017 and has not been replaced. The builders say in public that they do not know what is inside what they ship. Lem asked exactly the right question — what does a designer give up, and what must he refuse to give up? — and got the second half wrong, which is the one that mattered.</p>
    70	<p>The rest of this chapter mostly cannot be graded, and the ledger says so: seven of its eight rows are OPEN. That is not a failure of the grading, it is the finding. Lem is describing a working style here, not naming devices with dates, and a working style does not close. The one place he does commit to a dated order — traits orchestrated in fetuses long before an artificial chromosome apparatus — turns out to be unsettleable in a different way: neither side has happened, so the order cannot be scored at all. This is where the book's method runs out, and essay 0 will have to account for it. The temptation to score him anyway, by carrying his rules across to objects he never described, is exactly the failure this essay was rebuilt three times to avoid; the reader who thinks that is an excuse should attack this paragraph first.</p>
    71	
    72	<section class="open">
    73	<h2>Still open</h2>
    74	<ul>
    75	<li><strong>Whether mathematics ever gives the orders</strong> — the warehouse becoming "a phantomological generator, a creator of worlds" (p. 184). What would close it: a physical system built to instantiate a formal structure with no natural counterpart, and something useful done with it.</li>
    76	<li><strong>Whether the surplus is real.</strong> Can a system end with more structural information than went in? Two modern cases motivate the question without touching his accounting: superhuman play bootstrapped from rules alone, <!-- CHECK: alphazero-2018 --> and models degrading when trained on their own output. <!-- CHECK: model-collapse-2024 --> Neither is framed as a structural-information balance. What would close it: an accounting that distinguishes new structural information from re-encoded rules — or a proof that the accounting cannot be done.</li>
    77	<li><strong>Whether "useful" can be measured once the measurement is contaminated.</strong> This is the essay's transposition of his experiment rule, not a question he left open. What would close it: an evaluation regime whose scores demonstrably survive the systems trained against them — or a demonstration that every proposed regime degrades under optimisation, which would settle it the other way. <!-- CHECK: benchmark-crisis-2025 --></li>
    78	
    79	</ul>
    80	</section>
    81	
    82	<div class="reading">
    83	<h2>Reading</h2>
    84	<p><strong>Lem:</strong> "The Silence of the Designer" (pp. 176–179) is the chapter in miniature; "Methodological Madness" (pp. 179–184) is the mad tailor and the ladder; "Models and Reality" (pp. 186–190) is the apple; "Plagiarism and Creation" (pp. 190–192) is the side entrance. Ch. 4's "Belief and Information" (pp. 124–131) is the same argument in another key, and "The Beliefs of Electric Brains" (pp. 138–141) is the experiment nobody has run.</p>
    85	<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> three sources carry the argument. Sutton, "The Bitter Lesson" (2019), for the half of the Designer that arrived; Zhang et al. (ICLR 2017), for the falsified account of why the material works; and Gibson et al. (<em>Science</em>, 2010), for the side entrance actually taken. The rest is in the claims register.</p>
    86	</div>
    48	<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Object level</strong></td></tr>
    49	<tr><td>The visual field must change with intended head movement — "the impulses sent to his neck muscles" are "countered" (p. 202)</td><td>Head-tracked displays from Sutherland (1968) on: the head is tracked from outside and the view redrawn <!-- CHECK: sutherland-1968 --></td><td class="verdict v-ra">RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON</td></tr>
    50	<tr><td>Perfect imitation must give the eye "freedom also with regard to accommodation"; film's fixed focus won't do (pp. 198–199)</td><td>Fixed-focus optics remain the norm; the vergence–accommodation conflict is named and unsolved in shipping products <!-- CHECK: vac-unsolved --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    51	<tr><td>Phantomatics proper: bidirectional, "no 'exits'", indistinguishable (pp. 200–201, 207)</td><td>No shipped system meets the definition; varifocal prototypes (2018–) address one input-side problem; no dated path on the efferent, no-exits side <!-- CHECK: varifocal-prototypes --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    52	<tr><td>Later Lem, as Zylinska summarises the afterwords: VR devices "such as Eye Phone and Data Glove" among what he "did manage to predict correctly" (p. 21)</td><td>Head-worn tracked displays did arrive (row 1); on the full 1964 definition they are not phantomats (row 3); the afterwords' own wording is not in this edition</td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    53	<tr><td>"It is incomparably more difficult to construct the second one [human-producing] than the first [travel]" (p. 206)</td><td>Neither phantomat has been built, so the ranking is untested; text personas pass a five-minute test, but that is not the machine ranked <!-- CHECK: jones-bergen-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    54	<tr><td>Phantomatics "can mainly be a form of entertainment" (p. 214) — yet "its entertainment role may turn out to be rather marginal" (p. 215)</td><td>Hedged both ways about an unbuilt machine; the headsets that shipped are games-led, but they are not it <!-- CHECK: vr-use-mix --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    55	<tr><td>"a true blessing for the blind" whose "primary visual cortex" is intact (p. 215)</td><td>Cortical visual prosthesis in a six-participant early-feasibility trial <!-- CHECK: orion-cortical --></td><td class="verdict v-early">EARLY</td></tr>
    56	<tr><td>Teletaxy: a "remote-I" whose receptors and motor nerves are wired to yours (p. 222)</td><td>Motor read-out drives a robotic arm (2012); tactile feedback written into cortex (2021); both in trials, neither combined into a proxy body <!-- CHECK: bci-write-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-early">EARLY</td></tr>
    57	<tr><td>Cerebromatics: "impossible to add … absent traits" through such maneuvers (p. 216); a direct skill write "not simple even on a theoretical level" (p. 217)</td><td>Neural write in humans is stimulation and modulation; the impossibility is untested, not confirmed <!-- CHECK: bci-write-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    58	<tr><td>"We can expect that such a freezing technology, promising … 100 percent chance of a future revival … will be accomplished" (p. 231)</td><td>Organ-scale vitrification in rats, 2023; no whole mammal revived and no dated path from organ to organism <!-- CHECK: kidney-vitrification-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: alcor-1972 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
    59	<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Problem level</strong></td></tr>
    60	<tr><td>The cost of a fake scales with the complexity of the thing faked — Miss World cheaper than Einstein, interlocutor dearer than saucer (pp. 203, 206)</td><td>This essay's transposition: the fakes that fool people in 2026 are the cheapest ones — a warned judge loses a five-minute text test; the scarce resource was the receiver's credulity, not the simulator's complexity <!-- CHECK: jones-bergen-2025 --> <!-- CHECK: eliza-1966 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
    61	<tr><td>Indistinguishability belongs to feedback systems; one-way transfer is art (p. 200)</td><td>The mass "can you tell?" problem arrived in one-way generated images — the 2023 Pentagon fake <!-- CHECK: pentagon-fake-2023 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
    62	<tr><td>That one is in the real world "can always be only probable … but never absolutely certain" (p. 207)</td><td>The live skeptical literature holds the same (Chalmers 2022); Bostrom's 2003 argument is a related, different result <!-- CHECK: chalmers-2022 --> <!-- CHECK: bostrom-2003 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    63	<tr><td>The copy "is not the original, as it has a different history" (p. 229)</td><td>Common ground in the identity debate, Parfit included <!-- CHECK: parfit-1984 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    64	<tr><td>Therefore "telegraphing a person remains out of the question" (p. 229)</td><td>Parfit 1984 grants the premise and rejects the conclusion — identity is not what matters; unsettled <!-- CHECK: parfit-1984 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    65	</tbody></table></div>
    66	
    67	<section class="better">
    68	<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
    69	<p>Two places. First, on what a fake experience can do to a mind, he is more careful than most of the argument since. The phantomat "cannot be used to determine attitudes, opinions, beliefs, or feelings directly" (p. 213), <em>and</em> prolonged, staged exposure "can also, through appropriate maneuvers, lead to a change of opinion, even of a deep-rooted one," harder the more life a person has behind them (p. 217). The feed experiments, which are not his cases, at least do not contradict him: three months of chronological feeds on Facebook and Instagram moved nothing measurable, <!-- CHECK: guess-2023 --> and seven weeks of X's algorithm switched on shifted issue priorities but not partisanship or affective polarization. <!-- CHECK: gauthier-2026 --> His real worry is elsewhere — solitude: once you suspect the world, every check you run through another person is a move that teaches your opponent (p. 207). That is a sharper description of life inside a personalised system than "filter bubble," written before the feed. Second, the identity argument: his two scanners, twenty years before Parfit's teletransporter and branch line, are the same cases with the opposite conclusion, and the argument is still open.</p>
    70	</section>
    71	
    72	<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
    73	<p>He priced the wrong thing — for the problem as it actually arrived. He measured a fake by the complexity of the thing faked; the fake that fools people at scale in 2026 is a few sentences of text, because the receiver completes other minds on thin evidence, and the fake that does not exist is the one he thought cheap, the world. That is a verdict on his cost model, not on his ranking of two unbuilt machines. The narrower omission is that the chapter never proposes growing a general interlocutor by training on a text corpus at scale — language, in it, happens inside the vision; it is never what a machine is trained on. He also excluded the one-way image from the problem by classing it as art; deepfakes are the one-way image posing exactly his question. On the second half he was worse in a smaller way: reversible freezing "will be accomplished" is the chapter's cleanest MISS.</p>
    74	
    75	<section class="open">
    76	<h2>Still open</h2>
    77	<ul>
    78	<li><strong>Whether the travel phantomat arrives at all.</strong> What would close it: a system that is bidirectional across the sensorium and defeats adversarial reality tests — the squats, the drawer — not a comfortable headset with a market; Meta's losses are the current price of the experiment. <!-- CHECK: reality-labs-losses --></li>
    79	<li><strong>Whether the cheap interlocutor meets Lem's own bar</strong> — an autonomy that survives a long game with a clever opponent (pp. 206–207); five minutes is not that. What would close it: a model a domain expert cannot distinguish from a peer over weeks — or a demonstrated ceiling.</li>
    80	<li><strong>Whether central phantomatics gets built.</strong> Reward-centre stimulation exists as therapy, not as his "technology of facilitated ecstasy" (p. 210). <!-- CHECK: bci-write-2026 --> What would close it: a stimulation device sold or sought for pleasure — or a record of it tried and stopped.</li>
    81	</ul>
    82	</section>
    83	
    84	<div class="reading">
    85	<h2>Reading</h2>
    86	<p><strong>Lem:</strong> pp. 197–208 carry the argument; "The Limits of Phantomatics" (pp. 211–215) is his own hedge; then keep going — "Cerebromatics" (pp. 215–221) and "Personality and Information" (pp. 224–236) are the half that has aged best. Zylinska, p. 21, for the afterwords.</p>
    87	<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> Jones &amp; Bergen, "Large Language Models Pass the Turing Test" (2025); IDC's AR/VR tracker; Weizenbaum's 1966 ELIZA paper; Chalmers, <em>Reality+</em> (2022); Bostrom (2003); Parfit, <em>Reasons and Persons</em> (1984), part III; Guess et al., <em>Science</em> (2023) and Gauthier et al., <em>Nature</em> (2026) on feeds; Han et al., <em>Nature Communications</em> (2023) on the vitrified kidney.</p>
    88	</div>
    89	
